<article class="env-lemma" id="5E9F83">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/5E9F83">Lemma <span data-tag="5E9F83">4.2.3.3</span>.</a> Let $\calC $ be an $\infty $-category, and let $\sigma : \Delta ^ n \rightarrow \calC $ be a simplex having the property that $\sigma (0)$ is an initial object of $\calC $. Let $\bd \sigma = \sigma | \bd \Delta ^ n$. The natural map $\calC _{\sigma /} \rightarrow \calC _{\bd \sigma /}$ is a trivial fibration. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Unwinding the definition, we are reduced to solving the extension problem depicted in the diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { (\bd \Delta ^ n \star \Delta ^ m) \coprod _{ \bd \Delta ^ n \star \bd \Delta ^ m } (\Delta ^ n \star \bd \Delta ^ m) \ar [r]^-{f_0} \ar @{^{(}->}[d] &amp;  \calC \\ \Delta ^ n \star \Delta ^ m. \ar @{-->}[ur]^-{f} &amp;  } \]
</div>
    <p>
       We can identify the domain of $f_0$ with $\bd \Delta ^{n+m+1}$. Our hypothesis guarantees that $f_0(0)$ is an initial object of $\calC $, which in turn guarantees the existence of $f$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>